  class B,P,C,S,X step;
  class D,O decision;
  class I,R issue;
  class Z done;</pre></details></figure><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Required record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 · Baseline</td><td>Freeze the legacy query/transformation and representative output so the comparison is reproducible.</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Parallel run</td><td>Run the same use case against legacy and new sources over an agreed observation period.</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Compare and classify</td><td>Compare IDs, coverage, attributes, freshness and model output. Classify source truth, timing, market/grain mismatch, transformation defect or accepted semantic change.</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Sign-off</td><td>Squad lead approves tolerance results and documented exceptions; Product Owner accepts the use-case outcome.</td></tr><tr><td>5 · Reversible cutover</td><td>Switch analytical reads to the governed contract with a tested rollback and known support contact.</td></tr><tr><td>6 · Observe and find dependents</td><td>Watch agreed parity and service metrics; migrate reports or secondary pipelines that still use the old artefact.</td></tr><tr><td>7 · Retire</td><td>Decommission only the proven redundant analytical pipeline/store after the observation gate and dependency review.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="meta">Parity thresholds, observation-window length and rollback trigger are open and must be agreed with each squad before its parallel run.</p></section>
<section class="sheet" id="squads"><div class="section-kicker">Migration mapping</div><h2>What each squad would change</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Squad</th><th>Analytical baseline to compare</th><th>Proposed replacement</th><th>Specific proof</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Search &amp; PLP Data Science</td><td>Ad hoc Elasticsearch/Mongo-derived analytical joins.</td><td>Product–Article Current; Identifier Crosswalk for migration and exceptions; Product Attribute History for back-testing (UC-03).</td><td>Identity, catalog coverage and feature parity across representative search and listing use cases; history depth against the agreed retention (Search has asked for three years).</td></tr><tr><td>Recommendations Data Science</td><td>Daily BEAM snapshot; exact physical source still requires confirmation.</td><td>Product Attribute History; optional derived daily projection only if needed. Historical active/in-stock state (UC-06) also needs Stock/Availability history, a related domain planned for Wave 4.</td><td>Point-in-time reconstruction and model-input parity at the agreed cut-off. Use cases that depend on in-stock history stay on the legacy path until the Stock/Availability product passes parity.</td></tr><tr><td>Campaigns Data Science</td><td>Daily Databricks Volume export from Postgres/TimescaleDB.</td><td>Approved compatibility view over Article, SKU and Navigation contracts; related facts joined separately.</td><td>Campaign selection and hierarchy/identifier parity without embedding a temporary view as a permanent authority.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><strong>Partial cutover is expected.</strong> Wave 2 publishes descriptive contracts only. Recommendations’ historical active/in-stock use case (UC-06) and any Search back-testing beyond the approved retention period cannot fully migrate in Wave 3; those use cases remain on their legacy analytical paths until the related-domain products and retention decision (ADR-05) close the gap.</div><p>These mappings are proposed migration hypotheses. A physical dependency inventory and representative use-case replay are required before any squad’s legacy analytical path is retired.</p></section>
<section class="sheet" id="retirement"><div class="section-kicker">Safe decommissioning</div><h2>Retire a duplicate only when it is demonstrably redundant</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Required evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>All approved use cases and downstream dependents have an accepted replacement or documented exception.</td></tr><tr><td>Parity</td><td>Agreed output tolerance holds over the observation window, including historical and edge cases.</td></tr><tr><td>Service</td><td>Freshness, quality, access, alerting, support and recovery are operating at agreed targets.</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback</td><td>Fallback procedure is tested and an owner remains available until the cutover is declared stable.</td></tr><tr><td>Approval</td><td>Squad lead, Product Owner and BEAM operational owner sign off the specific artefact being removed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><strong>Scope boundary.</strong> This plan does not retire MongoDB, PostgreSQL or Elasticsearch serving for website/app runtime. A future operational replacement needs its own architecture decision. If parity fails, keep or restore the legacy analytical path while the difference is investigated.</div></section>
<section class="sheet" id="expansion"><div class="section-kicker">Wave 4 and beyond</div><h2>Expand through the same governed intake</h2><p>After the MVP, each attribute or related domain follows one repeatable route: profile the source, define grain and meaning, confirm authority and delivery interface, reconcile comparable values, approve contract impact, publish through the existing boundary, then monitor adoption and fallback use.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Candidate expansion</th><th>How it enters</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Remaining approved PIM / Enriched Product attributes</td><td>Promote from inventory through the attribute register and stable typed/long-form contract.</td><td>No automatic promotion of raw fields.</td></tr><tr><td>Navigation and categories</td><td>Validate Conform delivery, owner, completeness and market/category semantics.</td><td>Buying hierarchy remains separate unless a use case approves it.</td></tr><tr><td>Price, Stock/Availability and Promotions</td><td>Separate related-domain products with their own source owners, grains and SLAs.</td><td>Do not merge fast-changing facts into descriptive SKU identity.</td></tr><tr><td>Reviews, media and specialist data</td><td>Choose core attribute or related product according to grain and domain ownership.</td><td>Reviews remain a related product proposal.</td></tr><tr><td>Data Science derivations</td><td>Publish only with named definition, lineage and owner.</td><td>Keep derived features distinct from source-authored attributes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="sheet" id="ownership"><div class="section-kicker">Approval and evidence</div><h2>Who needs to accept the handover</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Required contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Product Owner</td><td>Initial and expansion scope, outcome, retention, MVP acceptance and analytical retirement.</td></tr><tr><td>Design Authority / Data Solutions Architect</td><td>Architecture route, canonical IDs/grains, ADRs and contract-change approval.</td></tr><tr><td>BEAM Engineering</td><td>Pipeline, data quality, history, monitoring, recovery, support handover and replay evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Data Governance</td><td>Definitions, source authority, lineage, classification, access and policy decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Domain/source owners</td><td>Source semantics, physical lineage, feed behaviour, discrepancies and change notification.</td></tr><tr><td>Data Science squad leads</td><td>Use-case selection, parity tolerance, exception acceptance and migration sign-off.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Approval package:</strong> agreed identifier profiling; authoritative attribute list; confirmed physical lineage; named owners; signed contract schemas; classification/access record; measured quality baselines; history/retention decisions; squad parity results; and active runbook/rollback evidence.</p></section>
<section class="sheet" id="risks"><div class="section-kicker">Delivery risk</div><h2>Risks to manage at the gates</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Gate response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>False Article-ID convergence</td><td>Profile both derivations across market and time before approving the crosswalk or contract key.</td></tr><tr><td>Raw Ingest becomes the contract</td><td>Require a private adapter and published boundary before consumer access.</td></tr><tr><td>Squad snapshot becomes source authority</td><td>Use it as comparison evidence; domain owners approve the business meaning.</td></tr><tr><td>The “22” becomes a permanent ceiling</td><td>Confirm the real initial list and fund a governed wider attribute inventory.</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed grains or fast-changing commerce facts distort identity</td><td>Keep Product, Article, SKU and related-domain outputs distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>Premature decommissioning</td><td>Require observed parity, dependent migration, rollback and explicit artefact-level sign-off.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="sheet" id="decisions"><div class="section-kicker">Still to decide</div><h2>Open items that affect sequence or dates</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Must be resolved</th><th>Blocks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="decision-id">ADR-01</span> Source route</td><td>Direct Ingest-to-private-DSO permission and governance controls.</td><td>MVP build route.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="decision-id">ADR-02/04</span> Identity</td><td>Canonical Product/Article/SKU keys and effective-dated raw mapping.</td><td>Comparable dataset and schema sign-off.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="decision-id">ADR-03/06</span> Scope and authority</td><td>Actual minimum attribute list, core/related boundary and per-field source precedence.</td><td>MVP contract and expansion.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="decision-id">ADR-05</span> History</td><td>Retention, cut-off, late arrivals, restatement, deletion and backfill.</td><td>History build and point-in-time acceptance.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="decision-id">ADR-07/08</span> Change and migration</td><td>Schema notice window, adapter review and temporary view retirement.</td><td>Consumer cutover and future releases.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="decision-id">ADR-09/10</span> Platform boundary</td><td>Operational scope, catalog/grants, quality implementation and recoverability.</td><td>Production readiness and retirement.</td></tr><tr><td>Consumer/service thresholds</td><td>Parity tolerance, observation window, refresh target, RPO/RTO, rollback trigger and support hours.</td><td>Squad sign-off and committed delivery dates.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="source-note">Source basis: <em>Roadmap &amp; Decision Register</em> (22 Sep 2026); <em>Personalisation Product Data Product Roadmap and Architecture</em> v1.3; <em>Product Data Product — Remaining Architecture Artifacts</em> v1.2; the DRD and current conflict register. Companion documents <a href="08-target-architecture-and-operations.html">08 · Target Architecture &amp; Operations</a>, <a href="06-output-contracts.html">06 · Output Contracts</a> and <a href="07-physical-schema.html">07 · Physical Schema</a> give deeper design detail; this roadmap can be read independently.</p></section>
